EXT. GENERAL STORE PORCH - MORNING

A dusty main street, a hitching rail, a water trough. Quiet. A wind chime of old spoons clinks.

SILAS CROWE, fifties, black hat, graying mustache, a stranger who sits on a barrel with a tin cup of coffee, looks up as BESSIE MARSH, forties, the store owner, sweeps the boards by the door, and LITTLE JOE TALLCHIEF, twelve, rolls a hoop down the street with a stick, and REV. ABERNATHY, sixty, a preacher in a rusty black coat, steps out of the church across the way and shades his eyes, all four of them noticing at the same instant the cloud of dust rising far out on the plains.

BESSIE
Riders.

SILAS
Four of them.

LITTLE JOE
(dropping the hoop)
How can you tell?

SILAS
Four plumes. Hush.

REV. ABERNATHY
(calling across the street)
Mrs. Marsh, is that the Dalton boys?

BESSIE
God help us if it is.

Silas stands, sets down his cup, and unbuttons his coat. Bessie leans the broom against the wall. Little Joe edges toward the porch steps. Rev. Abernathy crosses the road with surprising speed, his Bible clutched to his chest.

REV. ABERNATHY
(arriving)
Silas Crowe. I know you from somewhere.

SILAS
Never been here, Reverend.

REV. ABERNATHY
Springfield, 'seventy-eight. The tent revival.

SILAS
You must be thinking of another man.

BESSIE
He's thinking of the man on the wanted poster in my window.

SILAS
(a thin smile)
Mrs. Marsh, you're sharper than your broom.

LITTLE JOE
Mr. Crowe, are you an outlaw?

SILAS
I'm a retired one.

The dust cloud grows. The riders become shapes, then men. Their hats are low. Bessie and the Reverend both step back, and Little Joe slips behind the barrel, and Silas Crowe, still smiling, takes off his hat and sets it on the rail.

SILAS (CONT'D)
Ma'am, a cup of that coffee wouldn't hurt.

BESSIE
You expecting company?

SILAS
I expect they'll want to talk.

She pours. He drinks. Hooves slow at the hitching rail. Silas doesn't turn.

SILAS (CONT'D)
(softly)
Morning, boys.
